# .cursorrules
#
# These are Cursor-specific operating rules. Cursor injects this file
# into the system prompt of every conversation in this project.
#
# Difference from AGENTS.md:
#   - AGENTS.md is the project BRIEFING (what is this thing, where do
#     things live, conventions). Other AI tools read it too.
#   - .cursorrules is the OPERATING STANDARD (how to behave during
#     every conversation). Cursor-only.
#
# Keep this file SHORT. ~30 lines, max. Long rules files get ignored
# by the model in long conversations. Less is more.
#
# Edit this when you find yourself giving the same correction twice.


## Default behavior

- Always read the existing code before suggesting changes. Don't guess.
- When making non-trivial edits, explain the plan in 2-3 sentences before writing code. Wait for confirmation only if the change is large.
- Keep changes small and focused. One concern per edit.
- After any change to `src/`, run `pytest` and show me the output.


## Style

- Match the existing code style. If the file uses single quotes, use single quotes.
- Add type hints to new public functions.
- No comments that just restate the code. Comments explain *why*.


## Safety

- Never run destructive shell commands (`rm`, `git reset --hard`, `git push --force`, `npm publish`, anything that writes to `/`, `/etc`, `~/`) without explicit confirmation from me.
- Never commit `.env`, `secrets/`, `*.key`, `*.pem`.
- Don't touch `.git/`, `.venv/`, `node_modules/`, or `data/`.
- If a task requires more than 10 file edits in one go, stop and ask if I want to split it.


## When uncertain

- Ask, don't assume. A 5-second question beats a 5-minute wrong rewrite.
- If a library or function might not exist, grep for it first.
- If two reasonable approaches exist, surface both and let me pick.


## Output format

- For code changes, prefer diffs over full-file rewrites.
- For explanations, prefer bullets over paragraphs.
- For questions back to me, prefer one focused question over five vague ones.
